# Every change gets a WO number (Cindy, Aug 30 2026)

## The gap this closes

`loop-work-orders.mdc` describes the queue path: Cindy says "work the queue", you take the
oldest OPEN row, you finish with a DONE file + deploy-queue row + ledger update.

**That rule only fires in queue mode.** When Cindy asks for a fix conversationally
("the PO print margins are wrong, fix it"), you are not in queue mode, so no WO number is
allocated, no DONE file is written, and no ledger row is touched. Only the deploy-queue
line gets appended, because that is tied to deploying.

**Measured Aug 30, 2026: 39 deploy-queue rows since Aug 22 carry a build number. Zero carry
a WO number.** The last work order written was WO-121 on Aug 22. `loop/LOOP_LEDGER.md` still
ends at row 121. Builds 9.9.261 through 9.9.300 exist only as deploy lines.

The numbering did not break. It stopped.

## The rule

**A code change is a work order. There is no second category.** It does not matter whether
it came from the queue, from a chat message, from a screenshot, or from a bug Cindy hit
thirty seconds ago.

### Before you edit (same turn as the first edit)

1. Read `loop/LOOP_LEDGER.md`, take the next free number.
2. Add the ledger row: number, one-line title, state `IN PROGRESS`, executor, and a
   Notes field naming who asked and what the reported symptom was.
3. Say the number out loud in chat: *"This is WO-122."*

For a one-line fix this is thirty seconds. Do it anyway. The cost of skipping it is that
in three days nobody can tell which build changed the thing that is now broken, which is
exactly the position this repo is in today.

### When you finish

Unchanged from `loop-work-orders.mdc`, all three steps, every time:

1. `loop/WO-###_DONE_CR_[MonDD].md` — files touched with line ranges, what changed, self-test.
2. Append to `_DEPLOY_QUEUE.md`, **with the WO number in the line**, STATUS: pending.
3. Set the ledger row to `DONE-UNVERIFIED`. Never `VERIFIED` — that belongs to the verifier.

### Deploy-queue line format

The WO number goes immediately after the date stamp:

```
[2026-08-30 11:45] WO-122 #Cursor po-print-ship-tag-thumbs — <files> — staging — <what changed>. Studio 9.9.300. Verify: <doc>. — STATUS: pending
```

A deploy-queue row with no WO number is an incomplete row. If you are about to write one,
stop and allocate the number first.

## Do not

- Do not treat "this is too small for a work order" as a reason to skip. There is no size floor.
- Do not batch several unrelated fixes under one number. One change, one number.
- Do not write the DONE file later "when things calm down." It is written in the same turn
  the change is finished, or it is not written at all.
